1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now the old scout master.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Have you been to summer camp yet?

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
We're a week away and I'm looking forward to it.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
You know they say at camp everything is within walking distance.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
I suppose that's true, but theoretically the comedian Stephen Rice said

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
everything's really pretty much in walking distance if you have the time.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Hey, this is podcast number 179..

8
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Well, welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast.

9
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,000
This is Clark Green.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Let's take a look at the mailbag.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Our friend William D Levasque was kind enough to leave a very complimentary review

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
of the book Thoughts on Scouting.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Have you heard about the book?

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Got a book now, right with a cover and pages and all that stuff.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
He left a wonderful review on amazoncom where you can find the book,

16
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
He said. I feel this would be an excellent suggestion for someone making a transition

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
from cub scout leadership roles to becoming an adult leader in a scout troop.

18
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:39,000
This really does a nice job of suggesting to the reader how the adult leader role

19
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:45,000
in a troop must be different in order for boy scouts to grow and thrive.

20
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,000
If you care about becoming involved in scouting,

21
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
a copy of this book would make a very meaningful gift

22
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
If you're a volunteer searching for brief, high impact statements

23
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
to enhance training sessions or set the tone for meetings.

24
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
you'll find rich content here.

25
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
If you are personally looking for some material to refresh or re-energize

26
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
your commitment to serving youth through scouting, you're going to love this book.

27
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Well, thank you so much, William.

28
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
I really do appreciate that and I'm so glad that you enjoyed the book.

29
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Pete Parris is with Troop 41 in Junction City, Kansas,

30
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
and he wrote to say: Clark, your webpage is unbelievably fantastic.

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Either you have a staff of 100 people helping you or you never sleep.

32
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:32,000
I never sleep, Pete. That's the deal, because it's a staff of one.

33
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
The amount of scouting knowledge here is overwhelming.

34
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Please keep up the good work.

35
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Well, thank you so much, Pete, and we certainly appreciate hearing from you,

36
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
And, you know, spend a little time.

37
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Go over to the blog at scoutmastercgcom. this past week-

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Do you know at least where we are in the world? in the Mid-Atlantic,

39
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,000
we have had somewhere between two and three times the amount of rainfall that we're used to.

40
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
So we've been doing a little camping in the rain and because of that

41
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
I've published a couple of things this past week on the blog-

42
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Ten more tips for camping in the rain.

43
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
You want to make sure to check that out.

44
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
How to make a fire in the rain.

45
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,000
That's an infographic that we published this week

46
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
That should help you make it through the rainy season if you're in the same boat as we are,

47
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Literally.

48
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
I also posted an article about Bannock this week.

49
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
If you don't know what Bannock is, well, get over to scoutmastercgcom and check it out.

50
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
A short time ago, Wisconsin scoutmaster Gordon Bain sent me a copy of his new book.

51
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
It's a collection of 11 campfire stories for scouts

52
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
and it's called The Edge of the Firelight, and I want to recommend it to you.

53
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,000
These are kind of ghost stories and they're really cool.

54
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Imagine hunkering down around the fire and hearing a very credible account of someone you know or a place you've been,

55
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
with just enough factual information to draw you in, but with this kind of eerie twist to it

56
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,000
that raises the hair on the back of your neck.

57
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
and that's what Gordon has done with these campfire stories with his troop over the years

58
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
and with titles like The Missing Bugler and The Demon Troop.

59
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
These are real scouting stories.

60
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
These are ones that Gordon actually made up,

61
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:23,000
and so you can use these as kind of a model to tailor your own stories for your locality, for your scouts.

62
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
It's called The Edge of the Firelight.

63
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
You'll find a link where you can get the book in the post that contains this podcast.

64
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
This week we continue our light summer fair and we're going to answer some email questions.

65
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
That's going to take up the remainder of the podcast, so let's get started. shall we

66
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Write me a letter? send it by name

67
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Email. that is, folks.

68
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:59,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

69
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Hey, here's a great email I got from John.

70
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,000
He said I recently discovered your blog and podcast and I've enjoyed them a lot.

71
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
I signed some form and the next thing I know I was an assistant scoutmaster.

72
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Okay, so no big deal.

73
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
The scoutmaster was very gung-ho type and his two older boys worked well with the younger boys

74
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:22,000
and I wasn't really needed that much, except, you know, to drive occasionally.

75
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Then I was asked to be the acting scoutmaster.

76
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
I really don't understand that one, but I'm paying attention.

77
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Up until now the boys have been led by the adults and the adults did most of the planning

78
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
and told the boys where they were going and when to be there.

79
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
Now I've read your blog and sat through scoutmaster training at summer camp,

80
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:51,000
which, by the way, it's a lot of fun watching PowerPoint presentations in a 90-plus degree building.

81
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
I've read most of the scoutmaster manual and that pointed me to five other books to read

82
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
and even more meetings.

83
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
So I will tell my wife and daughter I love them and you know I have to keep a job,

84
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
so this is all a little daunting.

85
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
I've found it difficult to deal with other adults involved with the troop.

86
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
I have one that thinks we're still in wee-belows.

87
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
I have another whose son was in the troop ten years ago

88
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
and has some sort of district, something or another now,

89
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
and they keep telling me these boys need to toughen up,

90
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
that they're way behind other troops.

91
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
I don't understand how we're behind other troops.

92
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
From what I see of other troops, at least the really organized ones,

93
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
the adults are pushing their scouts in the direction they want them to go.

94
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Yeah, I wish we were that crackerjack troop with gourmet cooking skills in the woods,

95
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
but we're not and we may never be.

96
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
I wish they would cook something more than hot dogs and hamburgers.

97
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
but they like hot dogs and hamburgers

98
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
If it has cheese on it and they have a piece of fruit.

99
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:53,000
technically I guess it falls within the food pyramid. so there you go.

100
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:58,000
I really need to get them used to picking their own outings and activities-

101
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Things I wish they would do- and I can make suggestions.

102
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,000
I've told them it's up to them. as long as it follows the 12 points of the scout oath and law,

103
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:10,000
I won't put any obstacles in front of them that aren't already there.

104
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,000
I get tired of hearing that they want to play laser tag instead of going camping.

105
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
If they all decide for, an outing in the month is at the arcade.

106
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
I remind them that this helps no one advance in any way,

107
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,000
but I guess, if they still insist, that's what they want, what do you do?

108
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Do we just tell the assistant scout masters that a decision has been made by the boys

109
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
and we need to round up drivers?

110
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
I'm anxious to get the patrol leaders council going.

111
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
It seems like it's going to be a very slow process.

112
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,000
If they can get more than one meeting planned at a time, though,

113
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
I think I'll be happy.

114
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:45,000
I have a feeling that this acting scout master thing is going to become permanent

115
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
In our troop. we will have an election for this.

116
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
I'm going to tell anyone that wants me to keep the job

117
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
about the changes that I plan to make,

118
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
And if they don't like it, I guess they can vote for somebody else.

119
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
In fact, I'd really wish they would vote for somebody else.

120
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Is it possible to steer the scouts away from this type of video game camp out?

121
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,000
How much say does the scout master get in directing activities?

122
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Forgive me if my email has turned into something more of a rant than a question.

123
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:17,000
You know my wife does listen to me, but she has no idea what I'm talking about.

124
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:23,000
Well, John, I'm really glad you found me and I'm glad that you got in touch,

125
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,000
And I'm perfectly willing to listen to and read rants.

126
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:32,000
I've written and spoken many similar ones before.

127
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
The description of your troop and your situation- your fellow adult volunteers-

128
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
has much in common with other ones I hear about.

129
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,000
So first, and maybe the most important thought I have for you

130
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
is that you're not alone.

131
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:51,000
Okay, Being a scout master is a demanding job because it's important work.

132
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Training is good

133
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,000
And, for all love, why anyone thinks we should be using PowerPoint to do it is beyond me.

134
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:02,000
But, as you've discovered, developing as a volunteer is really up to you,

135
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
And training is just the first step.

136
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:09,000
You already know a lot about what scouting ought to be.

137
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,000
You've told me where your problem spots are,

138
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
And let me answer three questions for you.

139
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
First of all, is it worth the effort?

140
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Well, naturally I'm biased, but scouting has been worth the effort for me.

141
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:25,000
I don't actually know anyone who regretted being a volunteer scouter.

142
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
At times the work is kind of discouraging and it might be a little trying.

143
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:34,000
But as with most things, you get in, you get out of it what you put into it.

144
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,000
The second question I want to answer that you seem to imply here:

145
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
will you be able to make it happen?

146
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:41,000
Yes, you will.

147
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:45,000
Millions of scouts and scouters have made this happen over the past century.

148
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Nobody had a perfect troop or a perfect patrol.

149
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Nobody had perfect scouts.

150
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
Not one had a perfect record of outstanding service,

151
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
But in the end they got something right.

152
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Few of these people were any smarter or any better prepared than you are,

153
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,000
And their scouts were just a bunch of boys who did pretty average things.

154
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
And there were plenty of fits and starts along the way.

155
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
I don't know if you're still deciding about continuing,

156
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
but I think you're pretty solid on the whole enterprise.

157
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
It seems like you've caught the vision of what this ought to be,

158
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
but you aren't too sure if you can overcome the reluctance or resistance

159
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
of the other adults involved,

160
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:28,000
And you're not too sure if your scouts are really going to be able to rise to the challenge.

161
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:34,000
I'll tell you, even after 30 years, those doubts are still in my mind to some extent,

162
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
So don't let them deter you.

163
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
Just get with the program and get moving,

164
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
And things are going to go well.

165
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
What are the next three things that I think you should do?

166
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
Well? first, solidify your vision.

167
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:50,000
Continue to study and strive to understand what this is all about

168
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,000
And, as you do, commit this growing vision to writing

169
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
and sketch up a three or four-year plan

170
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
noting where things are now and where they ought to be.

171
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
You may never actually show this plan to anybody else,

172
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
but it just helps sort out your thinking.

173
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
I think it's a good idea to sit there and write it down.

174
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Don't worry about how these things are going to happen right now.

175
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Just write out what you want to see.

176
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:14,000
The second thing I would encourage you to do is define your terms

177
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
for continuing as Scoutmaster.

178
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
If there's some decision point as to who will be the Scoutmaster coming up,

179
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:24,000
you'll want to define what this means for you and for the troop.

180
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:28,000
A lot of this comes with the plan that you're going to be working on,

181
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
But there are some other questions to answer.

182
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
How much time will you be able to dedicate to this?

183
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
What will you be changing

184
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
And what are your expectations of support and help from others?

185
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
I think it's perfectly appropriate to spell things out

186
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and to have some well-defined expectations.

187
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
After all, that's what leaders do, right?

188
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:51,000
This is even more important in volunteer positions.

189
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:55,000
If you're taking the lead, you're expecting folks to follow,

190
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,000
not to undo what you're trying to achieve.

191
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:02,000
So making those things clear is very, very important.

192
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:06,000
The third thing I would do is I would develop a tolerance

193
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
for adversity and uncertainty

194
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,000
and an appreciation for incremental improvement.

195
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Measure your progress against where you started,

196
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
not what other people have achieved.

197
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
You won't be able to get everything done at once.

198
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Most things are going to take a little longer than you think

199
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:29,000
and they will likely fall flat a few times before they actually fly.

200
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
If you learn to recognize and build on incremental improvements

201
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
in your scouts and fellow volunteers,

202
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
you're going to see improvement everywhere you look.

203
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
You aren't starting at a negative and building towards a positive.

204
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:45,000
You're starting with abundant potential that will grow incrementally.

205
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:49,000
Some of the time it will feel like you're filling up a bathtub

206
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:53,000
with an eyedropper, but once in a while the dam breaks

207
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
and you move forward in some pretty big steps.

208
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Get these big ideas solidified in your head and your heart. first

209
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:02,000
You'll have a nagging feeling that you ought to be thinking

210
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:06,000
in more practical terms about the mechanics of making things work.

211
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
but there are plenty of mediocre, ineffective volunteers

212
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:15,000
who know every detail of the administrative and mechanical side of scouting.

213
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
I would say about two in ten scouts really understand

214
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
the big picture of working with boys

215
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:24,000
in a way that makes the promise of scouting come alive for them.

216
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Now you said, if they all decide they're outing for the month

217
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
at the arcade, what do I do,

218
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
You know? do I just have to go with whatever they want?

219
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
Well, no, you tell them to choose something that scouts do.

220
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
Scouts don't go to arcades.

221
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:41,000
Maybe they can do that as a patrol day trip or something,

222
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:46,000
but so far as the central activities of the patrol and the troop,

223
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:50,000
you need to guide them into doing things that scouts do,

224
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
and that's camping and hiking and biking

225
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
and canoeing and things like that.

226
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
You have a scout troop, right?

227
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Scout troops do these sorts of things.

228
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
If you had a football team,

229
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
you wouldn't show up with lacrosse sticks and expect to play lacrosse

230
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
If you want to join a lacrosse team, that's fine.

231
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:09,000
This is a football team. We play football,

232
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
You know. if the big thing in your life

233
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:14,000
is playing arcade games and laser tag and stuff like that,

234
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
we'll find a group that does that.

235
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:20,000
but this is a scout troop. We do scout stuff.

236
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
You're very right in theory

237
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
that scouts need to be able to make decisions and plans,

238
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
but I think the thing that you're missing

239
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:31,000
is they also do this in the context of scouting.

240
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:34,000
Scouts lead and decide and direct.

241
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
but it's not purposeless anarchy.

242
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:40,000
It has a point. Scouts do what scouts do

243
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
because by doing these things

244
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
they achieve the aims of scouting.

245
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,000
Scouts have no idea that that's going on, by the way.

246
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
They just think about camping and other stuff.

247
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
but we know better.

248
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
A scout master does guide his scouts

249
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
to obey the rules of the game and play within the boundaries.

250
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
This isn't a free-for-all Katie, bar the door

251
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:03,000
devil, take the high. most boys will be boys kind of thing.

252
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:07,000
That's what people are usually afraid is going to happen

253
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
when you turn things over to the scouts,

254
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,000
because they don't understand that there are rules

255
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
and boundaries to the game,

256
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
that there is a context in which we do our activities

257
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,000
and that is, we do what scouts do.

258
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
So, John, I hope that helps

259
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
and I look forward to hearing back

260
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:28,000
and getting some progress on your career as a scout master.

261
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:34,000
Brian Parton is with Troop 271 in Ocean Springs, Mississippi.

262
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:35,000
He says this:

263
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
your website and podcast are addictive.

264
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:40,000
I have a number of our leaders now tuning in and following you.

265
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,000
It's comforting to know that the issues and challenges happening

266
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
in our troop are not unique to us.

267
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
You're a great source of inspiration and wisdom.

268
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Well, thank you, Brian,

269
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
And hey, Troop 271 down in Ocean Springs,

270
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
It's good to have you listening.

271
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
A question came up recently at our meeting

272
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
about first-class requirement number three,

273
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
which is: since joining,

274
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,000
you need to participate in 10 separate troop

275
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:06,000
and patrol activities other than troop and patrol meetings,

276
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
three of which included camping overnight.

277
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,000
Demonstrate the principles of leave no trace

278
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
on these outings?

279
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
The issue we're having is whether the last sentence

280
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
demonstrate the principles of leave no trace

281
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
on these outings applies to camping overnight

282
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
or all 10 of the activities.

283
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
We use troop master for tracking advancement

284
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:29,000
and the program requires that the leave no trace box

285
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
gets checked in order for the activity

286
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
to be counted for the requirement.

287
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
Even though we understand that scouts

288
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
should always follow leave no trace.

289
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
not every activity is outdoors- camping in the wilderness-

290
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:44,000
where we commonly apply those leave no trace principles.

291
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
What's your take on this?

292
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,000
In that requirement, my reading is:

293
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:53,000
leave no trace applies to the terms these outings.

294
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
So a scout has to do 10 activities,

295
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
a minimum of three of the activities

296
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
involved camping overnight.

297
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
I think it's safe to say that the demonstration

298
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:07,000
of leave no trace applies to the camping trips.

299
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
But the whole discussion is a little moot

300
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
because, as you noted, there isn't any time

301
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
a scout would not practice leave no trace

302
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
when he was able to do so.

303
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
It's not as if we're going to tell our scouts

304
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
to demonstrate leave no trace three times

305
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
and then stop practicing it because you know

306
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
they've already got the badge right.

307
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
Every once in a while you run into some wording

308
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
and a requirement that causes a little bit of ambiguity.

309
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
You apply a little common sense

310
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
and you read it a couple of times,

311
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
discuss it with folks and you find your way out.

312
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
So I hope that helps.

